lesson:
Three tools for modelling data honestly.

**Enums: a fixed set of choices.** A direction is North, East, South or West. Storing it as a string invites `"Nort"` bugs; an `enum` makes the compiler check it:

```
public enum Direction { North, East, South, West }
Direction facing = Direction.North;
```

Enums pair beautifully with switch expressions: `facing switch { Direction.North => ..., ... }`. Under the hood each value is a number (North = 0, East = 1...), which is handy (`(Direction)(((int)d + 1) % 4)` turns right) but has a trap: `(Direction)9` compiles. Always end an enum switch with `_ => throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(...)`. When you parse text with `Enum.TryParse`, also check `Enum.IsDefined`, because `"7"` parses fine as a number.

**Records: data with value equality.** Two grid positions (2, 5) are the same position, but two `class` objects with the same fields are *different* objects to `==`. A `record` compares by value:

```
public record GridPos(int X, int Y);

new GridPos(2, 5) == new GridPos(2, 5)    // True
var moved = pos with { Y = pos.Y + 1 };   // a changed copy; pos is untouched
```

One line gives you a constructor, read-only properties, `==`, and a readable `ToString()` (`GridPos { X = 2, Y = 5 }`). Use records for **values**: positions, money amounts, settings, messages, save data. Use classes for **things with identity that change over time**: a player, an enemy, a bank account.

**Exceptions: when something can't be done.** You've thrown `ArgumentException` since Level 1. Now make your own when the problem means something in your program:

```
public class InsufficientFundsException : Exception
{
    public decimal Shortfall { get; }
    public InsufficientFundsException(decimal shortfall)
        : base($"Short by {shortfall}") { Shortfall = shortfall; }
}
```

The caller can catch exactly that case and use the extra information:

```
try { account.Withdraw(500); }
catch (InsufficientFundsException ex) { Console.WriteLine($"You need {ex.Shortfall} more"); }
finally { log.Add("withdraw tried"); }
```

- Catch the **specific** exception first, general ones after.
- `finally` runs whether things worked or not: closing files, saving logs.
- **The common mistake:** `catch (Exception) { }` with nothing inside. It swallows every bug, including ones you'd want to see. Catch only what you can actually handle.
- Exceptions are for exceptional cases. "Player pressed the wrong key" is normal; return a value. "Save file is corrupt" is exceptional; throw.

example.code:
using System;

public enum Direction { North, East, South, West }

public record GridPos(int X, int Y)
{
    public GridPos Step(Direction d) => d switch
    {
        Direction.North => this with { Y = Y + 1 },
        Direction.South => this with { Y = Y - 1 },
        Direction.East => this with { X = X + 1 },
        Direction.West => this with { X = X - 1 },
        _ => throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(d))
    };
}

public class OutOfMapException : Exception
{
    public OutOfMapException(GridPos p) : base($"{p} is off the map") { }
}

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        var start = new GridPos(0, 0);
        var p = start.Step(Direction.North).Step(Direction.East);
        Console.WriteLine(p);                              // GridPos { X = 1, Y = 1 }
        Console.WriteLine(p == new GridPos(1, 1));         // True: same values
        try
        {
            if (p.X > 0) throw new OutOfMapException(p);
        }
        catch (OutOfMapException ex) { Console.WriteLine(ex.Message); }
        finally { Console.WriteLine("checked the map"); }
    }
}

